## What an adapter must do (the suite expects it)

1. Call `precheck(nodes)` from src/precheck.ts first in load(). It returns
   { index: AddressIndex, nodes (first per address, document order), warnings: FormulaError[],
     errors: Map<Address, FormulaError>, formulas: Map<Address, { tokens, references }> }.
   - precheck refuses, before any engine parses: grammar errors, formulas over 8,192 characters, nesting over 64
     (parentheses plus runs of unary signs), functions outside the whitelist and the operators & and % (not-whitelisted),
     bare names (syntax), missing references (single ref absent; wildcard/range over an unknown item),
     ranges over 65,536 cells (syntax), and mixed currencies on money formula nodes (mixed-currencies).
   - Report precheck.warnings as your load warnings, plus your own: syntax from your parser, and one `cycle` warning
     per node ON a cycle (address = that node). Nodes downstream of a cycle read as {error:"cycle"} with no warning.
   - Nodes in precheck.errors must read as that error; any node reading them reads the same error kind.
2. Rewrite references with `formulas.get(address).references`: each has { start, end } (span of "[...]" in the
   formula, brackets included), text, kind "single"|"list", addresses (list: wildcard in document order, range
   row-major; a range may name cells with no node: treat them as empty cells, not missing).
3. Input conversion: type "date" values are full-date strings (engine serial = days since 1899-12-30); "money"
   values are decimal strings ("420.00") -> numbers; null = empty cell. read() of a "date" node returns a full-date
   string; money and number nodes return numbers (no rounding: display rounds to the minor unit).
4. Error kinds the suite compares (kind only, not address/detail): missing-reference, cycle, not-whitelisted, syntax,
   value (also Excel's #NUM!: overflow, SQRT(-1), 0^0, CEILING sign mismatch), division-by-zero, mixed-currencies.
   Load warnings are compared as the set of "kind@address".
5. A formula whose result is an empty cell reads 0; a list (wildcard) used where one value is expected is `value`.

